<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frisia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frisia is the coastal region along the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> inhabited by the Frisians, which in its widest early medieval extent reached from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zwin">Zwin</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. Conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the 8th century, its lands were later divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> province of Friesland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and North Frisia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>. Frisian languages are still spoken in parts of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frisia.jpg" alt="Frisia" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ætoms · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frisia_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
